# Dispatcher list - FreeSWITCH backends
# Format: setid  destination  flags  priority  attrs
#
# Flags:
#   0 - No flags (use default)
#   1 - Inactive (destination is down)
#   2 - Probing (being tested)
#   4 - Disable DNS SRV lookups
#   8 - Disable DNS A lookups
#   16 - Delete entry on expire
#
# Priority: HIGHER number = tried FIRST by alg 8 (serial/first-active).
#   VERIFIED against Kamailio 5.8 source — do NOT trust the old "lower =
#   higher priority" note that used to live here, it was wrong:
#   add_dest2list() keeps the set's linked list in ASCENDING priority order
#   (dispatch.c:589-604, "highest priority last -> reindex will copy
#   backwards") and reindex_dests() then copies that list BACKWARDS into the
#   dlist[] array (dispatch.c:803-816, j = nr-1 down to 0 walking from the
#   list head), so dlist[0] — the entry alg 8 selects first (hash=0,
#   dispatch.c:2466-2468) — is the destination with the HIGHEST numeric
#   priority. Corollary for EQUAL priorities: FILE ORDER IS REVERSED in
#   dlist[] (the last-listed line lands at dlist[0]) — never rely on file
#   order for precedence; always encode precedence in the priority number.
#
# Attrs: Key-value pairs for custom routing
#   weight=N - Relative weight for weighted distribution
#   maxload=N - Max concurrent calls (for call-load algorithm)
#   socket=udp:IP:PORT - Force specific local socket
#   duid=STRING - Unique destination ID
#
# Load balancing algorithms (used in ds_select_dst):
#   0 - Hash over Call-ID
#   1 - Hash over From URI
#   2 - Hash over To URI
#   3 - Hash over Request-URI
#   4 - Round-Robin
#   5 - Hash over authorization username
#   6 - Random
#   7 - Hash over PV
#   8 - Priority-based selection (use priority field)
#   9 - Weight-based (use weight attr)
#   10 - Call-load distribution (use maxload attr)
#   11 - Relative weight (percentage)
#   12 - Parallel forking to all

# ============================================================
# Group 1: Primary FreeSWITCH Media Servers
# ============================================================
# These handle all call processing - RCF, API Calling, SIP Trunk

# Primary FreeSWITCH instance (host networking)
# Weight: 100, MaxLoad: 2000 concurrent calls
# FS binds to the VM's internal VPC IP (local_ip_v4 = 10.142.0.100)
# so dispatcher must target that IP, not 127.0.0.1
#
# FS-AWARE HEALTH: this destination is ACTIVELY PROBED with OPTIONS every
# ds_ping_interval (5s) because ds_probing_mode=1 (probe ALL destinations,
# not just flagged/inactive ones — see kamailio.cfg). FreeSWITCH answers
# OPTIONS with 200 OK, so a healthy FS stays ACTIVE; a dead FS is marked
# inactive after ds_probing_threshold (3) failed probes (~15s). That
# transition fires event_route[dispatcher:dst-up|dst-down], which is matched
# by port ":5080" (unique to FS — all Bandwidth destinations are :5060) to
# maintain the fshealth=>fs_up flag consumed by GET /healthz and the
# SIP-honest OPTIONS handler. The flags field stays 0: with ds_probing_mode=1
# no explicit "probing" (2) flag is needed, and leaving it 0 keeps the long-
# standing behavior byte-identical. Do NOT set flag 1 (inactive) here.
#
# STRICT ACTIVE/STANDBY FS PAIR (optional, env-gated — mirrors the SBC pair
# model): when FREESWITCH_IP_2 is set in the SBC .env, entrypoint.sh renders
#   - the FS-1 line's templated priority field as 10  (ACTIVE:  dlist[0])
#   - the FS-2 placeholder below as a real line, priority 5 (STANDBY: dlist[1])
# and kamailio.cfg's route[DISPATCH] switches ds_select_dst group 1 from
# alg 4 to alg 8 (serial / "use always first entry", dispatch.c:2466). Alg 8
# always starts at dlist[0] and only walks past a destination whose state is
# INACTIVE or DISABLED (ds_skip_dst, dispatch.h:51 — note TRYING is NOT
# skipped, so probe failures below ds_probing_threshold do NOT move traffic).
# Net effect: FS-1 serves ALL new calls while healthy; FS-2 runs fully hot
# (probed by the same OPTIONS ping, DB-connected, sofia up) but receives ZERO
# new calls; when FS-1 is marked INACTIVE, every new call goes to FS-2; when
# FS-1 recovers (ds_inactive_threshold successful probes) new calls
# automatically fail back to FS-1. NOT round-robin, NOT load-sharing.
# When FREESWITCH_IP_2 is unset, the FS-1 priority field renders back to 0
# and the placeholder renders as a comment — byte-identical single-FS
# behavior.
1 sip:__FS_IP__:5080 0 __FS1_PRIORITY__ weight=100;maxload=2000;duid=fs-primary
__FS2_DISPATCHER_LINE__

# For horizontal scaling beyond the active/standby pair, add further lines
# with DESCENDING priority numbers (each lower number = later in the alg-8
# serial order — see the priority note at the top of this file):
# 1 sip:freeswitch-3:5080 0 3 weight=100;maxload=2000;duid=fs-tertiary

# ============================================================
# Group 2: Bandwidth Primary (Dallas)
# ============================================================
# Bandwidth Dallas signaling IP — primary carrier for all products.
# Mapped from X-Carrier: primary in route[TO_CARRIER].
#
# PURPOSE: Dispatcher sends periodic SIP OPTIONS to this IP
# (controlled by ds_ping_interval). This serves two functions:
#   1. NAT keepalive — Keeps the SIP signaling NAT pinhole open
#      through GCE's 30-second UDP idle timeout. Without periodic
#      SIP traffic, the NAT mapping expires and inbound SIP from
#      Bandwidth (new INVITEs, re-INVITEs) may not reach Kamailio.
#   2. Health monitoring — Detects carrier unreachability early.
#      If Bandwidth stops responding to OPTIONS, ds_probing marks
#      the destination inactive and logs a warning.
#
# NOTE: This group is for probing/keepalive ONLY. Actual outbound
# call routing to Bandwidth is handled by route[TO_CARRIER] using
# $rd/$du directly — NOT via ds_select_dst on this group.
2 sip:67.231.2.12:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-dallas-primary

# ============================================================
# Group 3: Bandwidth Secondary (LA)
# ============================================================
# Bandwidth LA signaling IP — secondary/failover carrier for all products.
# Mapped from X-Carrier: secondary in route[TO_CARRIER].
# Same keepalive/health-monitoring purpose as Group 2.
3 sip:216.82.238.134:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-la-secondary

# ============================================================
# Group 4: Bandwidth TC1 - GraniteTelecommunicationsLLC_01 (Default)
# ============================================================
# TC1 signaling IPs: New York and Atlanta.
# Same keepalive/health-monitoring purpose as Groups 2-3.
# NOT used for call routing — actual routing is in route[TO_CARRIER].
# IPs templated by entrypoint.sh from BANDWIDTH_TC1_NY / BANDWIDTH_TC1_ATL
# (defaults: 67.231.9.142 / 67.231.13.185 — same values as before templating).
4 sip:__BANDWIDTH_TC1_NY__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-tc1-ny
4 sip:__BANDWIDTH_TC1_ATL__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-tc1-atl

# ============================================================
# Group 5: Bandwidth TC2 - GraniteTelecommunicationsLLC_02
# ============================================================
# TC2 signaling IPs: Dallas and Los Angeles.
# Same keepalive/health-monitoring purpose as Groups 2-3.
# NOT used for call routing — actual routing is in route[TO_CARRIER].
# IPs templated by entrypoint.sh from BANDWIDTH_TC2_DAL / BANDWIDTH_TC2_LA
# (defaults: 67.231.1.188 / 67.231.4.138 — same values as before templating).
5 sip:__BANDWIDTH_TC2_DAL__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-tc2-dal
5 sip:__BANDWIDTH_TC2_LA__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=bw-tc2-la

# ============================================================
# Group 6: Sinch Origination - Denver (DNVTCOZIGR2_3278)
# ============================================================
# Sinch Denver signaling IP — ORIGINATION-ONLY second carrier (inbound
# INVITEs round-robin to all 3 zone NLB VIPs; outbound/termination stays
# Bandwidth). Same keepalive/health-monitoring purpose as Groups 2-3:
# OPTIONS probes keep the GCE UDP NAT pinhole open and give the
# carrier-monitor sidecar up/down visibility (CARRIER_SETIDS includes 6-7).
# NEVER used for call routing — Sinch is not a TO_CARRIER destination.
# IP templated by entrypoint.sh from SINCH_DENVER_IP (default 206.146.100.24
# — test TN 5305480845).
6 sip:__SINCH_DENVER_IP__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=sinch-denver

# ============================================================
# Group 7: Sinch Origination - Chicago (CHCGIL24GR4_7412)
# ============================================================
# Sinch Chicago signaling IP — same origination-only/keepalive notes as
# Group 6. IP templated by entrypoint.sh from SINCH_CHICAGO_IP (default
# 206.146.101.39 — test TN 5305480846).
7 sip:__SINCH_CHICAGO_IP__:5060 0 0 weight=100;duid=sinch-chicago

# ============================================================
# Notes:
# ============================================================
# - Kamailio will probe each destination every ds_ping_interval seconds
# - Destinations that fail ds_probing_threshold probes are marked inactive
# - Inactive destinations are retried and reactivated after ds_inactive_threshold successes
# - Use ds_mark_dst("p") to manually mark a destination for probing
# - Use ds_mark_dst("i") to manually mark a destination as inactive
# - Use ds_mark_dst("a") to manually mark a destination as active
#
# To reload dispatcher list without restart:
#   kamcmd dispatcher.reload
#
# To view dispatcher status:
#   kamcmd dispatcher.list
